  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran's ambassador to Russia, Kazem Jalali, published a June 8 statement in Izvestia announcing that the Strait of Hormuz will reopen under an Iran-Oman fee framework, with fees varying by vessel type and cargo. This is the first official Iranian articulation of reopening terms since the June 1 suspension of talks, and it comes from a senior official — though the US government immediately rejected the fee premise and Washington had earlier warned Oman against participating in toll-setting arrangements. The IRGC has simultaneously continued intercepting vessels attempting transit outside the fee regime. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span> (incorporated in F5 reduction to 0.22); supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>CNN published an analysis on June 8 headlined "The ultimate hostage negotiation: Why Iran talks are deadlocked," drawing on an exclusive statement by Mohsen Rezaei — military adviser to Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei — that "the negotiations are at a deadlock and Trump must break this deadlock." Rezaei's demand of $12 billion upfront from the $24 billion in frozen Iranian assets has not been met; Trump's camp has not publicly accepted this precondition. This statement came three days after the June 5 horizon date of F2, confirming the no-deal resolution, and shapes the probability space for F8. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (RESOLVED-NO); supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>On June 7–8, Israel and Iran exchanged what CNN described as the "worst strikes in months," with Iran launching missiles toward Israel in stated retaliation for Israeli strikes on Beirut. Despite this military escalation, a top Iranian official (identified as Ebrahim Azizi) told CNN that Tehran has "no problem" with peace talks in principle, and Trump posted on June 8 that "final negotiations on Peace are proceeding." This tension between active military exchange and ongoing diplomatic maneuvering is the defining feature of the current deadlock: neither side has chosen full escalation, but neither has accepted the other's financial terms. Lebanon remains a complicating variable — Hezbollah rejected the June 3–4 ceasefire deal, with further talks not scheduled until June 22. Azizi's conditional statement and Trump's unilateral assertion do not meet F4's resolution criteria (IRNA/Tasnim confirmation or named mediator readout required); both indicators NOT_OBSERVED. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (−0.10 to 0.35)</p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill stands at first reading (106-0, June 2) with no election date yet inserted. Netanyahu and coalition partners appear to have informally converged on an October 20 date rather than September; October 20 is earlier than October 27 and would satisfy both F6's resolution criterion and F7's early-dissolution clause. The bill's window (Sept 8–Oct 20) was agreed in committee but formal insertion of a date is reserved for the second and third readings. No second or third reading has been scheduled as of June 9. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span> (+0.02 to 0.52), moved <span class="cite">F7</span> (+0.10 to 0.45)</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:37%;">Escalation 37%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:40%;">Protracted 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:23%;">De-esc 23%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (37%):</strong> Talks fail to resume before June 16 (p≈0.65 from F4), no deal by July 4 (p≈0.75 from F8), and Israel-Lebanon military exchanges persist — producing a compounding probability of ~0.37 in which Hormuz remains shut, economic pressure escalates, and the risk of miscalculation rises with each exchange.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (40%):</strong> Some diplomatic contact is re-established — possibly after the June 22 Lebanon round — but content deadlock over frozen assets and Lebanon persists, Hormuz stays below 20 vessels/day through July, and both sides manage military escalation below the threshold of full-scale resumption; the most probable single outcome at current probability levels.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (23%):</strong> F4 resolves YES (talks resume), F8 and F9 move toward YES in tandem (deal signed, corridor opens), and the Lebanon track stabilizes enough for Pezeshkian's channel to outmaneuver the Rezaei faction — a path that requires the $24B impasse to break and Lebanon to stabilize before June 22.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's internal political situation over the reporting period (June 2–9) is dominated by the tension between the hardline Rezaei faction and the pragmatic diplomatic channel. Rezaei — military adviser to Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei and former IRGC commander — gave CNN an exclusive on June 5 in which he framed the Hormuz standoff explicitly as leverage: $24 billion in frozen assets as the price for reopening. This framing contradicts the posture of negotiators like Araghchi, who had been closer to the May 28 MOU. Pezeshkian and Ghalibaf were earlier reported by Iran International (April 30) to have sought Araghchi's removal on charges of "subservience" to the Guards, a development that reflected internal competition between the president's office and the IRGC-linked faction over the pace and terms of any deal. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Ambassador Jalali's June 8 statement in Izvestia on transit fees represented a possible convergence within the Iranian establishment around a formula for re-opening that preserves some degree of Iranian sovereignty and economic benefit from the strait — positioning a toll not as capitulation but as a new permanent regime. That this was stated to a Russian outlet (not through Pakistan or Oman, the active mediators) may reflect an attempt to test international reaction while maintaining deniability in the US-Iran channel. The US rejection of fees as "tolls" and Oman's cautious positioning after the earlier US warning against involvement in toll-setting create a structural gap between Iran's reopening formula and what Washington will accept. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span> without move</p>

      <p>The ceasefire between Iran and Israel was fraying badly on June 7–8 per CNN's live updates, with both sides trading their heaviest exchanges in months. From Tehran's perspective, continued Israeli strikes on Beirut and Lebanon provide domestic political cover for the Khamenei-circle hardliners to block any deal that does not first address the Lebanon front. The June 3–4 Israeli-Lebanese conditional ceasefire — which Hezbollah rejected — has left the Lebanon trigger unresolved; further Lebanon talks are not scheduled until the week of June 22. Until a Lebanon arrangement holds, the hardline faction retains a veto argument over re-engagement that the Pezeshkian government cannot easily override. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

